{
  peppy_schema: "contract/v1",
  manifest: {
    name: "scene_manipulation",
    tag: "v1",
    description: [
      "Editing the world of a simulation engine while it runs: listing the assets the engine can place, loading a scene, spawning, moving, resizing, pushing and removing objects, and relocating a robot. Any engine that keeps a scene graph can implement it; a commander only needs the contract, not the engine.",
      "Asset ids come from get_assets_list and carry their kind as a prefix (\"scene/<id>\", \"object/<id>\" or \"template/<id>\"). A template is a place that load_template builds anew at each seed: a room or one of several rooms of one kind, the furniture laid out by rules and the props drawn, so each seed gives another layout and other props, and the same seed the same world. Object ids are minted by the implementer on spawn, and by the implementer that places the objects of a loaded scene as objects of the same set. Each object also has a name. A spawned object has the name spawn_object gave it, until the object is removed. An object the loaded scene placed, a scene object, has the placement id the scene authors, such as \"mantel_clock_01\", which each load of that scene gives it again, where its object id is new. A name is lowercase letters, digits and \"_\". No two objects share a name, and each placement id of the loaded scene stays taken, also after its scene object is removed. get_objects_list reports what stands in the scene and where, and get_object reports one object at one instant, its landmarks in the world frame and its state, for a caller that sets the world up, checks its setup and checks an event it asked for; the engine's object_state contract reports the same objects with their velocities, as a stream, for evaluation. Robot names come from get_robots_list. Every position, orientation and vector of this contract is in world, the one frame of the simulation, and an answer names world in frame and the simulated instant of its values in timestamp, and an answer whose values hold at every time holds no timestamp. A request with no frame names world, a request in another frame is refused with `unknown_frame`, and a request's timestamp, given or left out, places its value in world at any time, since world does not move. Positions are x, y, z in meters, orientations unit quaternions [x, y, z, w], a heading an orientation that turns about +z alone, forces newtons, masses kilograms. An orientation whose squared norm lies 0.01 or more from 1 is refused with `rejected`, the tolerance of tf2's buffer that transforms names; the implementer normalizes each orientation it takes.",
      "Each scene entry and each object entry of a catalogue release has a version, 16 lowercase hexadecimal digits. The live version of an asset is the entry that get_assets_list gives for it. A release also holds retired versions: the entries of the releases before it that are live no more and that it keeps, so that a world that names one builds the asset as that entry gives it. The asset id of a scene or of an object is \"<kind>/<id>\", which names the live version, or \"<kind>/<id>@<version>\", which names the entry of that version, live or retired. get_asset, load_scene and spawn_object take an asset id with a version, and so does build of simulation_world, in each asset of its world document. Each other member that takes an asset id takes one with no version, and refuses one with a version as an id that names no asset: load_template, whose template id names no version, preview_asset and turntable_model of scene_view, and find_places and furnish of scene_workspace. An asset id with a version that the catalogue release does not hold is refused with version_not_held by each member that takes an asset id with a version, and the message names the version and the asset. A world can hold two versions of one asset: each object keeps the version that it was built with. Each answer that names the asset of an object or of the scene gives the asset id with no version: get_objects_list, get_object and get_scene, and the answers of scene_view, scene_workspace, object_controls, object_state, scene_lighting and scene_daylight. Only the setup record of simulation_world names the versions. So get_asset with the asset id that get_objects_list or get_object gives for an object describes the live version of its asset, also for an object of a retired version, and is refused when the catalogue lists that asset no more: get_object gives the landmarks of the object itself, and describe_workspace of scene_workspace its work surfaces. An implementer whose catalogue has no versions gives none, and refuses each asset id with a version as an id that names no asset.",
      "A snap, which spawn_object and move_object take, places an object so that one of its belt ends continues a belt end of another object. Each end is a landmark of use \"inlet\" or \"outlet\" (get_object lists an object's landmarks, get_asset an asset's) whose outer edge, on its axis behind an inlet's centre and ahead of an outlet's, is where its belt ends. The two edges meet, centred on each other, the two belts running away from the joint on either side of it, and the two belt tops level: the placed object stands upright, turned about the world's +z, its origin at the height of the other object's origin, and the implementer sets the control that raises the body of its end, a displacement along +z such as a conveyor's height, to the value that brings its end to the other's height. Things then run from one belt onto the other across an inlet snapped to an outlet when both belts run forward, and across two inlets or two outlets when one of them runs backward. A goal with a snap is refused with `rejected` for an object id that names no object, a landmark the object or the asset does not declare, a landmark whose use is neither \"inlet\" nor \"outlet\", two ends whose widths (their size across) differ by more than 0.001 m, an end that does not face up, and an end of the placed object that no control brings to the other's height within its range. An implementer that keeps no landmark refuses every snap with `rejected`. A snap that needs a control the implementer cannot set where the placed object stands fails with `precondition_failed`, and a snap whose ends the implementer finds apart once it placed the object fails with `attempt_failed`.",
      "A goal that this contract refuses with a kind fails with that kind when the implementer finds the cause only once the goal started. An implementer that lets no caller edit or inspect its scene answers each call it does not serve with `rejected`: an error reply to a service, a refusal to a goal.",
      "An implementer may keep placement rules, such as how deep a placed thing may sink into what stands around it and how near two robots may stand. A spawn_object, move_object or move_robot whose place the rules refuse, and a resize_object whose size they refuse where the object stands, fails with `precondition_failed`, and the message says why.",
      "An implementer may keep a texture budget: the most texture memory that a world shows, so that the page that shows it stays within what a browser holds. The texture memory of a world is the sum, over each texture that it shows once, of the bytes that the page uploads for it with its mip chain, the light of its room included. get_assets_list gives the texture memory of each object in texture_memory_bytes, the bytes that the object adds to a world that shows none of its textures. The textures of the deck of a riser of scene_workspace count one time for a world that holds a riser, however many risers it holds. A spawn_object that would take the world past the budget fails with `precondition_failed`, as do a stand_at and a raise_object of scene_workspace whose riser would, and load_template refuses a drawn world past it with seed_refused; each message names the budget, the memory of the world and what the call adds.",
    ],
  },
  failure_kinds: {
    seed_refused: "The seed gives no world of the world pattern: a sampler finds no value, a name that an entry of an item kind adds is a scene object name of the drawn scene, or the simulation refuses the drawn world; the message names the entry",
    version_not_held: "The catalogue release does not hold the version that the asset id names; the message names the version and the asset",
    unknown_frame: "A frame of the request is not world, the one frame of the simulation",
  },
  interfaces: {
    services: [
      {
        name: "get_assets_list",
        description: "Give the catalogue of placeable assets in brief, as a JSON array in assets_json: get_asset gives the full entry of one asset. The list holds the live version of each asset alone, and no retired version. Each request field narrows the list; a field that is absent, or sent empty, narrows nothing, so a request without fields lists every live entry of the catalogue.",
        request_message_format: {
          kind: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "\"scene\", \"object\" or \"template\". Another value gets an error reply with `rejected`." },
          category: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "A category get_asset_categories reports. An unknown one lists nothing." },
          query: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "Text an entry's asset_id, display_name or description must contain, compared without regard to case." },
          work_surface_kind: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "A kind of work surface, such as \"table\" or \"shelf\": lists the objects that carry a work surface of that kind alone, and no scene and no template. An unknown one lists nothing." },
        },
        response_message_format: {
          assets_json: {
            $type: "string",
            $description: "The catalogue as a JSON array of brief entries. Every entry carries at least asset_id, display_name, description, kind (\"scene\", \"object\" or \"template\") and category; implementers may add engine-specific fields that each hold one number, string or boolean, and add no field that holds a list or an object: get_asset gives those. An implementer whose catalogue has versions gives the live version of each scene and of each object in version, a string as the contract description defines it, beside an asset_id with no version; a template entry has no version. An implementer that keeps the front of an object gives it in front, the side of its root that a person faces when they use it (\"+x\", \"-x\", \"+y\" or \"-y\"), for an object that has one. An implementer that keeps a texture budget gives the texture memory of each object in texture_memory_bytes, as the contract description defines it.",
          },
        },
      },
      {
        name: "get_asset",
        description: "Give the full entry of one asset of the catalogue, as a JSON object in asset_json: the fields of a brief entry of get_assets_list, and the fields that hold a list or an object, which get_assets_list leaves out. An asset_id with no version gives the live entry of the asset, and the asset_id of a scene or of an object with a version gives the entry of that version, live or retired. An asset_id that names no asset of the catalogue gets an error reply with `rejected`, and one with a version that the catalogue release does not hold an error reply with version_not_held.",
        request_message_format: {
          asset_id: { $type: "string", $description: "An asset id that get_assets_list gives: \"scene/<id>\", \"object/<id>\" or \"template/<id>\"; or the asset id of a scene or of an object with a version, \"<kind>/<id>@<version>\"." },
        },
        response_message_format: {
          asset_json: {
            $type: "string",
            $description: "The entry as a JSON object: every field that a brief entry of get_assets_list carries, the version of a scene or of an object among them, beside an asset_id with no version, and the engine-specific fields that hold a list or an object. An implementer that keeps landmarks gives an object's landmarks (landmarks: each with its name, use, shape, body, centre, normal, axis and size, in the frame of its body at scale 1) and its work surfaces (work_surfaces: each with its landmark, its kind and the sides a robot works it from, as scene_workspace defines a work surface). It gives a scene's work surfaces, those of the objects the scene places, in work_surfaces: each with the placement id of its object (an object id exists only once the scene loads), the display_name of the object's asset, its landmark, its kind, the height of its top and, for each of its sides, the centre of the side's edge, in the world with the scene loaded at scale 1. It gives a scene's walls in walls: each with its id, the line of its inner face seen from above from [x, y] to [x, y] with the room on its left, its height and its openings, each a doorway (from_m, to_m along the wall from its start, and top) or a window (from_m, to_m, bottom and top), in the world with the scene loaded at scale 1; a scene without walls gives none. It gives a template's rooms in rooms, the asset id of each scene that load_template draws its room from, and its stations in stations: each kind of work surface that the stations of the template work, as kind, with how many stations work one, as count, the stations of its furniture and its fixed stations alike.",
          },
        },
        failure_kinds: ["version_not_held"],
      },
      {
        name: "get_asset_categories",
        description: "Give the categories of the catalogue and how many assets each one holds, so a caller filters get_assets_list before it lists.",
        response_message_format: {
          categories: {
            $type: "array",
            $items: {
              $type: "object",
              kind: { $type: "string", $description: "\"scene\", \"object\" or \"template\"." },
              category: "string",
              count: { $type: "u32", $description: "Assets of this kind in this category." },
            },
          },
        },
      },
      {
        name: "get_objects_list",
        description: "Give what stands in the scene and where, for a caller that sets the world up and checks its setup: every object the implementer tracks, spawned by a caller or placed by the loaded scene, with its pose as of `timestamp`. A dynamic object can move after that instant. The order is the implementer's object order.",
        request_message_format: {
          source: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "\"spawned\" lists the objects a caller spawned and the risers that their stand or their raise of scene_workspace left, \"scene\" the objects the loaded scene placed, \"riser\" the risers that a stand or a raise of scene_workspace placed and that belong to it. Absent, or empty, lists all of them. Another value gets an error reply with `rejected`. An implementer that tracks no scene-placed object as an object answers \"scene\" with an empty list, and an implementer that places no riser answers \"riser\" with an empty list." },
          category: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "A category get_asset_categories reports. Absent, or empty, lists every category." },
        },
        response_message_format: {
          frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames." },
          timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold." },
          objects: {
            $type: "array",
            $description: "Every object, each with the pose of its origin in the answer's frame: position, and orientation, how it is turned.",
            $items: {
              $type: "object",
              object_id: { $type: "string", $description: "The id move_object, resize_object, remove_object and apply_force take." },
              name: { $type: "string", $description: "The object's name, as the contract description defines it: the name spawn_object gave it, the placement id of an object the loaded scene placed, or, for a riser of scene_workspace, the name of its robot or of its object followed by \"_riser\", as scene_workspace says." },
              asset_id: { $type: "string", $description: "The \"object/<id>\" asset the object is an instance of, with no version; empty for a riser of scene_workspace, which is an instance of no asset." },
              category: { $type: "string", $description: "The asset's category, as get_asset_categories reports it; \"fixtures\" for a riser of scene_workspace." },
              source: { $type: "string", $description: "\"spawned\", \"scene\" or \"riser\"." },
              physics: { $type: "string", $description: "\"dynamic\", \"static\" or \"none\", as spawn_object takes it." },
              mass: { $type: "f64", $description: "Mass (kg)." },
              scale: { $type: "f64", $description: "Uniform scale; 1 = as authored." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
            },
          },
        },
      },
      {
        name: "get_object",
        description: "Give one object at one instant, for a caller that sets the world up, checks its setup and checks an event it asked for: its pose, the pose of its origin as get_objects_list gives it, its landmarks in the world frame and its state, all as of `timestamp`. Each state field is present only for an object whose asset simulates it. An object_id that names no object gets an error reply with `rejected`.",
        request_message_format: {
          object_id: { $type: "string", $description: "The id get_objects_list gives." },
        },
        response_message_format: {
          frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames." },
          timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold." },
          object_id: "string",
          name: { $type: "string", $description: "The object's name, as get_objects_list gives it." },
          asset_id: { $type: "string", $description: "The \"object/<id>\" asset the object is an instance of, with no version; empty for a riser of scene_workspace." },
          position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
          orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
          scale: { $type: "f64", $description: "Uniform scale; 1 = as authored." },
          landmarks: {
            $type: "array",
            $description: "The working parts of the object that its asset declares, in the world frame: each at the pose its body has now and sized by the scale, its centre in position. Empty for an asset that declares none. A riser of scene_workspace gives its base and its deck: the deck gives its size and its height.",
            $items: {
              $type: "object",
              name: { $type: "string", $description: "Unique in the asset, such as \"base\" or \"zone_front_left\"." },
              use: { $type: "string", $description: "\"base\" (the face the object rests on), \"support\" (a face that things rest on or in, such as the inside bottom of a pan or a cooker zone), \"opening\" (the mouth of a container), \"grip\" (a handle), \"inlet\" or \"outlet\" (where a belt takes or gives things) or \"tip\" (the end of a spout or a tool)." },
              shape: { $type: "string", $description: "\"point\", \"disc\", \"rectangle\" or \"bar\"." },
              body: { $type: "string", $description: "The body of the object the landmark is fixed to; \"root\" for the object's root body." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              normal: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "The unit vector it faces out of the object along: down for a base, up for a support, out of the mouth for an opening, the side a hand comes from for a grip, up from the belt for an inlet or an outlet, the way a tip points." },
              axis: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "A unit vector at a right angle to normal, along which the first side of size lies: the first side of a rectangle, the length of a bar, the direction of travel at an inlet or an outlet; any such vector for a disc or a point." },
              size: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 2, $description: "Along axis, then across it (m): a disc's diameter twice, a bar's length and thickness, [0, 0] for a point." },
            },
          },
          bounds: {
            $type: "object",
            $optional: true,
            $description: "The world-frame box of every colliding geometry of the object now: its body, its loose pieces and its contents. Its lowest x, y, z and its highest x, y, z (m). Absent for an object with no colliding geometry.",
            min: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3 },
            max: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3 },
          },
          breakage: {
            $type: "object",
            $optional: true,
            $description: "For an asset with pieces that break off: how many have broken off, of how many. The object is broken when pieces_off is above 0.",
            pieces_off: "u32",
            pieces_total: "u32",
          },
          contents: {
            $type: "array",
            $optional: true,
            $description: "For an asset that holds a liquid: one entry for each phase of the liquid, with the centre of its mass in position.",
            $items: {
              $type: "object",
              phase: { $type: "string", $description: "The phase's id, such as \"yolk\"." },
              mass: { $type: "f64", $description: "Its mass now (kg). It drops as water boils off." },
              in_shell: { $type: "f64", $description: "The share of that mass inside the shell, 0 to 1; 0 for an object without a shell." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              spread: { $type: "f64", $description: "The radius around the centre, in the horizontal plane, that holds 95 % of that mass (m)." },
              below: {
                $type: "object",
                $description: "What the phase rests on: the object itself while its shell holds most of the phase, else what a ray straight down from the centre meets first past the whole object, named as scene_view's locate_in_view names a thing.",
                kind: { $type: "string", $description: "\"object\", \"robot\", \"floor\" or \"backdrop\"; \"\" when the ray meets nothing." },
                object_id: { $type: "string", $description: "For an object: its object_id." },
                asset_id: { $type: "string", $description: "For an object: its \"object/<id>\" asset." },
                robot: { $type: "string", $description: "For a robot: its name." },
                part: { $type: "string", $description: "For a robot: the part the ray meets, an arm or a gripper under the name the robot's limbs answer to, or \"body\" for the rest of the robot." },
                name: { $type: "string", $description: "For \"backdrop\": the name the scene gives the piece." },
              },
              set: { $type: "f64", $description: "How far the phase has set, a mean over its mass, 0 to 1." },
              browned: { $type: "f64", $description: "How far the phase has browned, a mean over its mass, 0 to 1." },
              temperature_c: { $type: "f64", $description: "Its temperature, the mean over its mass (degrees C)." },
            },
          },
          membranes: {
            $type: "array",
            $optional: true,
            $description: "For each phase of the contents that a membrane holds, such as a yolk: whether the membrane is whole.",
            $items: {
              $type: "object",
              phase: "string",
              whole: "bool",
            },
          },
          crush: {
            $type: "array",
            $optional: true,
            $description: "For an asset with a part that crushes, one entry for each such part.",
            $items: {
              $type: "object",
              body: { $type: "string", $description: "The body that crushes." },
              crushed: { $type: "bool", $description: "True once its load passed the load that crushes it. What is crushed stays crushed." },
              crushed_by: { $type: "f64", $description: "How far its rest point moved from where it started: metres along a slide, radians about a hinge." },
            },
          },
          heat: {
            $type: "object",
            $optional: true,
            $description: "For an object that takes and gives heat: its temperature now.",
            temperature_c: { $type: "f64", $description: "Degrees C." },
          },
          heaters: {
            $type: "array",
            $optional: true,
            $description: "For an object with heaters, one entry for each heater: the temperature of what it heats directly, the glass of an induction zone or the air of an oven.",
            $items: {
              $type: "object",
              name: { $type: "string", $description: "The heater's name, such as \"zone_front_left\" or \"oven\"." },
              temperature_c: { $type: "f64", $description: "Degrees C." },
            },
          },
        },
      },
      {
        name: "get_scene",
        description: "Give the scene that is loaded now: the \"scene/<id>\" asset load_scene took, with no version, also after a load that named one, or the implementer's default scene after clear_scene, and the scale it stands at. An implementer that opens on no scene answers an empty asset_id until a load.",
        response_message_format: {
          asset_id: "string",
          scale: { $type: "f64", $description: "Uniform scale of the scene; 1 = as authored." },
        },
      },
      {
        name: "get_robots_list",
        description: "Give the robots standing in the world, one entry each, so a commander lists what it may address and a test counts what the world holds.",
        response_message_format: {
          frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames." },
          timestamp: { $type: "time", $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold." },
          robots: {
            $type: "array",
            $description: "One entry per robot, with the pose of its base in the answer's frame: position, where its base stands, and orientation, which way it faces, a turn about +z alone.",
            $items: {
              $type: "object",
              robot: { $type: "string", $description: "The instance name that addresses this robot, which is what move_robot takes." },
              model: { $type: "string", $description: "The id of the engine's robot catalogue it stands as." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
              attached: { $type: "bool", $description: "True for a robot that joined over simulation_robot, false for a robot the world stands itself, which leaves only with the simulation." },
            },
          },
        },
      },
    ],
    actions: [
      {
        name: "load_scene",
        description: "Replace the current scene with the asset named by asset_id, which must be of kind \"scene\". An asset_id with no version loads the live version of the scene, which places the live version of each of its objects; an asset_id with a version loads the entry of that version, live or retired, and the objects that a retired version places take the versions that its entry names. Spawned objects are removed first. A load_scene or a load_template that arrives while another one runs may replace it: the replaced goal then ends cancelled, with a reason that names the load that replaced it. A build of simulation_world that comes before the load has handed its command over ends it cancelled the same way, naming the build. The load is refused with `rejected` for an asset_id that names no scene of the catalogue and for a scale the implementer does not take, and with version_not_held for an asset_id with a version that the catalogue release does not hold, and fails with `attempt_failed` when the scene does not load. The feedback gives one message when the goal is accepted, then one whenever a field of the feedback changes, a few changes of a short period in one message, and none while nothing changes: silence means the goal makes no progress. The stream ends with the goal. The result is empty.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            asset_id: "string",
            scale: { $type: "f64", $description: "Uniform scale applied to the scene; 1 = as authored." },
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            bytes_fetched: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes of the scene's asset files received so far; 0 for an engine that fetches nothing." },
            files_ready: { $type: "u64", $description: "Files of the scene that are ready: found in the engine's cache or received whole." },
            building: { $type: "bool", $description: "True once every file is ready and the engine builds the scene." },
            bytes_ready: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes of the scene's asset files that are in the engine's cache: each file found there or received whole, and the bytes received so far of each file in transfer, each byte counted once. bytes_ready of bytes_total is the share of the fetch that is done." },
            bytes_total: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes of every asset file the scene needs, as the catalogue records them; 0 for an engine that fetches nothing." },
            bytes_per_second: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes received per second over the last two seconds, the rate of bytes_fetched; 0 once nothing arrived for two seconds, and once the engine builds the scene." },
          },
        },
        result_service: {},
        failure_kinds: ["version_not_held"],
      },
      {
        name: "load_template",
        description: "Load the template that template names at a seed, and set its world up in place of the current scene: a room of the template, its furniture laid out by the rules of furnish of scene_workspace, its props drawn, and each robot that stands at a joining spot of the room. The seed is the seed of seeded; without seeded, the implementer draws a seed, and draws again while a drawn seed gives no world, at most 10 draws in all, and with seeded it draws no other seed. The implementer first takes, for each robot that stands, in the order the robots joined, the joining spot of the drawn room that a join without a placement would take: when the room has a free spot for fewer robots than stand, the goal fails with `precondition_failed` before any change, naming the robot without a spot. It then expands the world pattern of the template at the seed as expand of simulation_world expands a pattern, apart from the live world: the layout avoids each robot at its spot as it avoids a robot that stands, the box seen from above of the robot's colliding geometry at its start pose at that spot, and a robot blocks no walkway and no doorway; an implementer may draw the objects of an item kind among some of the assets of the kind alone, and says which. It fetches the files of the drawn world, loads its room, moves each robot to its spot, places each piece of furniture and each prop of the world with its physics, and lets the objects settle for 2 s of simulated time. Each piece and each prop is then an object of the setup as spawn_object makes one, so the setup record of simulation_world holds a concrete world that a build makes again without the template. The drawn world takes the live version of its room and of each of its objects, and the setup record names these versions. Refused with `rejected` for a template the implementer does not hold, and by an implementer that lets no caller edit its scene. A seed that gives no world fails with seed_refused before any change, naming the entry: a drawn world past the texture budget of the contract description is such a world, and the message names the budget, the memory of the world and what the template adds. Files that do not come fail with `attempt_failed`, and the world is unchanged. After its first change, the goal fails as build of simulation_world fails after its first change: with `precondition_failed` when the world refuses a step, with `attempt_failed` for any other cause, and each robot that it moved but did not place goes back where it stood. A load_scene, a load_template or a build of simulation_world that arrives while it runs replaces it as it replaces a load_scene. An implementer that holds no template refuses each goal with `rejected`. The feedback is that of load_scene, over the files of the drawn world. The result gives the seed, the name of the drawn world, and each station of the world.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            template: { $type: "string", $description: "The template, by the asset id that get_assets_list gives it: \"template/<id>\", which names no version." },
            seeded: {
              $type: "object",
              $optional: true,
              $description: "When present, the world of this seed; absent, the implementer draws the seed.",
              seed: { $type: "u32", $description: "The seed of the world: the same template and seed always give the same world." },
            },
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            bytes_fetched: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes of the drawn world's asset files received so far; 0 for an engine that fetches nothing." },
            files_ready: { $type: "u64", $description: "Files of the drawn world that are ready: found in the engine's cache or received whole." },
            building: { $type: "bool", $description: "True once every file is ready and the engine builds the world." },
            bytes_ready: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes of the drawn world's asset files that are in the engine's cache: each file found there or received whole, and the bytes received so far of each file in transfer, each byte counted once. bytes_ready of bytes_total is the share of the fetch that is done." },
            bytes_total: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes of every asset file the drawn world needs, as the catalogue records them; 0 for an engine that fetches nothing." },
            bytes_per_second: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes received per second over the last two seconds, the rate of bytes_fetched; 0 once nothing arrived for two seconds, and once the engine builds the world." },
          },
        },
        result_service: {
          response_message_format: {
            seed: { $type: "u32", $description: "The seed of the world; 0 when the goal ends cancelled and built nothing." },
            world: { $type: "string", $description: "The name of the drawn world, <template id>_seed_<seed>; empty when the goal ends cancelled and built nothing." },
            frame: { $type: "string", $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames." },
            stations: {
              $type: "array",
              $description: "Each station of the world, where a robot stands to work a work surface: the stations of its furniture, in the order of the names of their pieces, then its fixed stations, in the order the template lists them. Each gives position, the place in front of the side, the centre of the station on the floor, and orientation, the heading toward the work surface from the station, a turn about +z alone, in the result's frame; a station holds at every time, so the result holds no timestamp. Empty when the goal ends cancelled and built nothing.",
              $items: {
                $type: "object",
                object_id: { $type: "string", $description: "The object whose work surface the station works, as get_objects_list gives it." },
                name: { $type: "string", $description: "The name of that object: a piece of furniture or a fixture of the room." },
                landmark: { $type: "string", $description: "The landmark of the work surface." },
                side: { $type: "string", $description: "The side of the work surface that the station stands at: \"+x\", \"-x\", \"+y\" or \"-y\", for stand_at of scene_workspace." },
                position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
                orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w]." },
              },
            },
          },
        },
        failure_kinds: ["seed_refused"],
      },
      {
        name: "clear_scene",
        description: "Remove every spawned object and restore the default scene. It fails with `attempt_failed` when the default scene does not load. The action gives no feedback and an empty result.",
        goal_service: {},
        result_service: {},
      },
      {
        name: "spawn_object",
        description: "Place one instance of an \"object\" asset and report its new id and its name. An asset_id with no version takes the live version of the asset, also in a world that holds a retired version of it, and an asset_id with a version takes the entry of that version, live or retired; the object keeps the version that it was built with. The goal gives target or snap_to. The spawn is refused with `rejected` when it gives both or neither, when the asset is unknown or when the arguments are invalid, and with version_not_held for an asset_id with a version that the catalogue release does not hold. It fails with `precondition_failed` when the placement rules that the contract description names refuse its place, or when the object would take the world past the texture budget that the contract description names, and with `attempt_failed` when the object does not spawn. A build of simulation_world that comes before the spawn has handed its command over ends it cancelled, with a reason that names the build, and it spawns nothing. The feedback gives one message when the goal is accepted, then one whenever a field of the feedback changes, a few changes of a short period in one message, and none while nothing changes: silence means the goal makes no progress. The stream ends with the goal. The result gives the id and the name of the new object.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            asset_id: "string",
            target: {
              $type: "object",
              $optional: true,
              $description: "Where the object stands: the pose of its origin in world; absent when the goal gives snap_to. A target with no orientation stands it as authored, as [0, 0, 0, 1] does. An orientation whose squared norm lies 0.01 or more from 1 is refused with `rejected`, the tolerance of tf2's buffer that transforms names; the implementer normalizes each orientation it takes.",
              frame: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames; absent, world." },
              timestamp: { $type: "time", $optional: true, $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold. A command leaves it out for the latest time; a report leaves it out for values that hold at every time." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $optional: true, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w], or absent for none." },
            },
            scale: { $type: "f64", $description: "Uniform scale; 1 = as authored." },
            physics: { $type: "string", $description: "\"dynamic\" (simulated, falls and collides), \"static\" (collides, never moves) or \"none\" (visual only)." },
            mass: { $type: "f64", $description: "Mass (kg) of the object, which only a dynamic object simulates: a finite number above zero, or 0 for the mass of a real one at its scale, the mass that the catalogue entry of the asset gives times the cube of scale. A mass of 0 for an asset to which the catalogue gives no mass is refused with `rejected`, as is a mass below zero or one that is not a finite number." },
            name: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "The object's name, of the form the contract description gives and free by its rules: a name of another form, one that an object holds, and a placement id of the loaded scene are refused with `rejected`, and a name that another spawn takes while this one fetches its files fails it with `rejected`. Absent or empty, the implementer names the object from its asset, such as \"crate\" or \"crate_2\"." },
            feeder: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "The feeder that spawns the object, by a name of the form the contract description gives, for a spawn that is not setup: the simulation keeps such a spawn out of its setup record (simulation_world). A feeder of another form is refused with `rejected`. Absent or empty for a spawn of the setup." },
            snap_to: {
              $type: "object",
              $optional: true,
              $description: "When present, the object stands where the snap that the contract description defines places it; absent when the goal gives target.",
              object_id: { $type: "string", $description: "The object whose belt end this one continues: an id get_objects_list gives." },
              landmark: { $type: "string", $description: "That object's landmark at its belt end, by name." },
              own_landmark: { $type: "string", $description: "The landmark of the asset that meets it, by name." },
            },
          },
        },
        feedback_topic: {
          qos_profile: "standard",
          message_format: {
            bytes_fetched: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes of the object's asset files received so far; 0 for an engine that fetches nothing." },
            files_ready: { $type: "u64", $description: "Files of the object that are ready: found in the engine's cache or received whole." },
            building: { $type: "bool", $description: "True once every file is ready and the engine builds the object." },
            bytes_ready: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes of the object's asset files that are in the engine's cache: each file found there or received whole, and the bytes received so far of each file in transfer, each byte counted once. bytes_ready of bytes_total is the share of the fetch that is done." },
            bytes_total: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes of every asset file the object needs, as the catalogue records them; 0 for an engine that fetches nothing." },
            bytes_per_second: { $type: "u64", $description: "Bytes received per second over the last two seconds, the rate of bytes_fetched; 0 once nothing arrived for two seconds, and once the engine builds the object." },
          },
        },
        result_service: {
          response_message_format: {
            object_id: { $type: "string", $description: "The implementer-minted id of the new object; empty when the goal ends cancelled and built nothing." },
            name: { $type: "string", $description: "The object's name: the one the request gave, or the one the implementer gave it; empty when the goal ends cancelled and built nothing." },
          },
        },
        failure_kinds: ["version_not_held", "unknown_frame"],
      },
      {
        name: "move_object",
        description: "Teleport an object to a new pose, zeroing its velocity. It keeps its orientation when the target gives none. The goal gives target or snap_to. The move is refused with `rejected` when it gives both or neither, for an object_id that names no object, for a riser while its stand or its raise of scene_workspace lasts and for an orientation that spawn_object refuses. A riser that its stand or its raise left moves as a spawned object does. It fails with `precondition_failed` when the placement rules that the contract description names refuse the place, and with `attempt_failed` when the object does not move. The action gives no feedback and an empty result.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            object_id: "string",
            target: {
              $type: "object",
              $optional: true,
              $description: "Where the object goes: the pose of its origin in world; absent when the goal gives snap_to. A target with no orientation keeps the orientation the object has; another is validated as spawn_object validates it.",
              frame: { $type: "string", $optional: true, $description: "The frame of the record's values, as transforms names frames; absent, world." },
              timestamp: { $type: "time", $optional: true, $description: "The time on the clock of transforms at which the values hold. A command leaves it out for the latest time; a report leaves it out for values that hold at every time." },
              position: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 3, $description: "x, y, z (m)." },
              orientation: { $type: "array", $items: "f64", $length: 4, $optional: true, $description: "A unit quaternion [x, y, z, w], or absent for none." },
            },
            snap_to: {
              $type: "object",
              $optional: true,
              $description: "When present, the object moves where the snap that the contract description defines places it; absent when the goal gives target. An object never snaps to itself.",
              object_id: { $type: "string", $description: "The object whose belt end this one continues: an id get_objects_list gives." },
              landmark: { $type: "string", $description: "That object's landmark at its belt end, by name." },
              own_landmark: { $type: "string", $description: "The landmark of the moved object that meets it, by name." },
            },
          },
        },
        result_service: {},
        failure_kinds: ["unknown_frame"],
      },
      {
        name: "resize_object",
        description: "Change the size of an object where it stands, and end once the world holds it at its new size. The goal gives scale, for an instance of an asset, or height_m, for a riser of scene_workspace, and the other one is 0. An instance of an asset, spawned by a caller or placed by the loaded scene, takes its new uniform scale: each of its lengths changes by the ratio of the new scale to the scale it has, and its mass by the cube of that ratio, so its density stays. Its origin moves along z alone, so that its lowest point stays at its height: the object does not sink into what holds it up and does not stand above it. An object of which nothing collides has no such point, and its origin stays where it is. It keeps its orientation and stands still at its new size. A part of it that turns keeps its angle, and a part that slides keeps its share of its travel. Each control of it that a caller set to a length or to a speed changes by that ratio, within the bounds of the control at the new scale. The other objects and the robots keep their state. The resize ends the raise of the object and each stand at a work surface of it, as a move of the object does: each riser of them stays where it stands, as a spawned object. A riser takes its new height, the height of the top of its deck above its origin, and its origin stays where it stands. A riser that its stand or its raise left changes each side of its deck by the ratio of the new height to the height it has. A riser that belongs to a stand or a raise keeps its deck, which is built to the base of its robot or to the footprint of its object, and changes its height alone: its robot stands again on a riser of that height at the same side of its work surface, with the same offset, as stand_at of scene_workspace stands it, or its object stands again on a riser of that height with what it carries, as raise_object of scene_workspace stands it, and the resize is refused and fails as that stand or that raise is. The resize is refused with `rejected` when scale and height_m are both above 0 or neither is, for an object_id that names no object, for a scale given for a riser, for a height_m given for an instance of an asset, for a scale that is not a finite number from 0.01 to 100, for a height_m that is not from 0.10 m to 0.80 m, the heights of a riser, for a riser that its stand or its raise left whose deck would have a side shorter than 0.20 m, for an object that the loaded scene joins to another one by a weld or a connect joint, and for a part of a room, such as a wall, a floor or a ceiling. A robot is no object, and no resize changes it. It fails with `precondition_failed`, and the world stands as it stood, when the placement rules that the contract description names refuse the object at its new size where it stands, for an object with a piece that broke off, for an object whose liquid left its shell, and for a size at which a sprung part of the object is not stable. It fails with `attempt_failed` when the world does not build with the object at its new size. An implementer that resizes no object refuses each goal with `rejected`. The action gives no feedback. The result gives the size the object now has.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            object_id: { $type: "string", $description: "The object to resize, by the id get_objects_list gives: a spawned object, an object of the scene or a riser of scene_workspace." },
            scale: { $type: "f64", $description: "For an instance of an asset: its new uniform scale, from 0.01 to 100; 1 = as authored. 0 for a riser." },
            height_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "For a riser of scene_workspace: its new height, from 0.10 to 0.80 (m). 0 for an instance of an asset." },
          },
        },
        result_service: {
          response_message_format: {
            scale: { $type: "f64", $description: "The uniform scale the object now stands at; 1 for a riser; 0 when the goal ends cancelled and resized nothing." },
            width_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "For an instance of an asset with a front: its width across its front. For one without a front: its longer side across. For a riser: the side of its deck along its own x. In metres; 0 for an object that takes no room, and when the goal ends cancelled and resized nothing." },
            depth_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "For an instance of an asset with a front: its depth from its front to its back. For one without a front: its shorter side across. For a riser: the side of its deck along its own y. In metres; 0 for an object that takes no room, and when the goal ends cancelled and resized nothing." },
            height_m: { $type: "f64", $description: "The height of the object in its own frame, and for a riser the height of the top of its deck above its origin, in metres; 0 for an object that takes no room, and when the goal ends cancelled and resized nothing." },
          },
        },
      },
      {
        name: "remove_object",
        description: "Delete an object. An object_id that names no object, and a riser while its stand or its raise of scene_workspace lasts, are refused with `rejected`. The action gives no feedback and an empty result.",
        goal_service: {
          request_message_format: {
            object_id: "string",
          },
        },
        result_service: {},
      },
      {
        name: "apply_force",
        description: "Push a dynamic object with a constant force in world, the request's frame, for duration_s seconds. The goal completes once the push is scheduled, not when it ends. A push on an object_id that names no object or an object that cannot move, with a force that is not finite, or with a duration the implementer does not take, is refused with `rejected`. The action gives no feedback and an empty result.",
        goal_service: {
